<body>
  <main class="container">
    <header>
      <h1>How to Look Up a Divorce Case in Cook County, IL</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">
        A step-by-step guide to searching Dissolution of Marriage records through the Clerk of the Circuit Court of Cook County.
      </p>
    </header>

    <ol class="step-list">
      <li class="step-item">
        <div class="step-number">1</div>
        <h2>Gather Your Search Information</h2>
        <p>Before starting your search, collect at least one of the following details:</p>
        <ul class="sub-list">
          <li><strong>Case Number:</strong> Cook County divorce case numbers typically include the 4-digit filing year, the letter <code>D</code> (for Domestic Relations), and the sequence number (e.g., <code>2024 D 012345</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Party Names:</strong> The full legal first and last name of either spouse (Petitioner or Respondent). Knowing the approximate year of filing helps narrow common names.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>

      <li class="step-item">
        <div class="step-number">2</div>
        <h2>Go to the Official Cook County Clerk Website</h2>
        <p>
          Visit the Clerk of the Circuit Court of Cook County's online portal at
          <a href="https://www.cookcountyclerkofcourt.org/online-case-information" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            cookcountyclerkofcourt.org/online-case-information
          </a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Follow the link to access the <strong>Online Case Information / Full Electronic Docket Search</strong> tool. (Note: Illinois-registered attorneys can also use the <em>CCCPortal</em> link on the Case Look Up page.)
        </p>
      </li>

      <li class="step-item">
        <div class="step-number">3</div>
        <h2>Select the Domestic Relations Division</h2>
        <p>
          All divorce (Dissolution of Marriage), legal separation, and civil union dissolution cases in Cook County are handled by the <strong>Domestic Relations Division</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          When prompted to choose a court division or search category, select <strong>Domestic Relations</strong> so your results are filtered to family and divorce filings.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li class="step-item">
        <div class="step-number">4</div>
        <h2>Enter Your Search Criteria</h2>
        <p>Choose your preferred lookup method on the search screen:</p>
        <ul class="sub-list">
          <li><strong>Search by Case Number:</strong> Enter the year, division code (<code>D</code>), and case number exactly as formatted by the prompt.</li>
          <li><strong>Search by Name:</strong> Enter the party's <strong>Last Name</strong> and <strong>First Name</strong>. If results are too broad, filter by filing date range.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Complete any required security verification (CAPTCHA) and submit your search.</p>
      </li>

      <li class="step-item">
        <div class="step-number">5</div>
        <h2>Review the Case Summary &amp; Docket Entries</h2>
        <p>Click on the matching case number from the search results to view free public docket information, including:</p>
        <ul class="sub-list">
          <li>Filing date and current case status (active, disposed, or closed)</li>
          <li>Assigned judge, courtroom, and courthouse location</li>
          <li>Scheduled future court dates and past hearing history</li>
          <li>A chronological list of motions, orders, and judgments entered</li>
        </ul>
      </li>

      <li class="step-item">
        <div class="step-number">6</div>
        <h2>Request Official or Plain Document Copies (If Needed)</h2>
        <p>
          The free online lookup displays <strong>docket summaries only</strong>—full PDF images of petitions, marital settlement agreements, and divorce decrees are not downloadable through the public web search. To obtain actual copies:
        </p>
        <ul class="sub-list">
          <li>
            <strong>In Person:</strong> Visit the Domestic Relations Division Clerk's Office at the <strong>Richard J. Daley Center</strong> (50 W. Washington St., Room 802, Chicago, IL 60602) or the suburban district courthouse where the case was heard (Skokie, Rolling Meadows, Maywood, Bridgeview, or Markham). Bring a valid photo ID and the case number.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>By Mail:</strong> Send a written request with both parties' names, case number (or approximate filing year), a self-addressed stamped envelope, and a check or money order for per-page/certification fees to the Clerk of the Circuit Court.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Helpful Tip:</strong> Sealed or impounded divorce files cannot be viewed by the general public without a court order. If you need assistance finding a case number or checking copy fees, call the Domestic Relations Division at <strong>(312) 603-6300</strong> or the Main Clerk's Call Center at <strong>(312) 603-5030</strong> (Monday–Friday, 8:30 a.m. – 4:30 p.m.).
    </div>

    <footer>
      <p>Disclaimer: This guide is for informational purposes only and is not legal advice. Always verify current fees and courthouse hours directly with the Clerk of the Circuit Court of Cook County.</p>
    </footer>
